# micro7 - 3x7 digits for a HUD margin too narrow for the digits family.
#
# A game draws its HUD into whatever margin its field leaves, and on the 64px
# panel the tetris field leaves 20px each side. Five figures need 44px in
# digits10 and 29px in sans8, so both of those cuts truncate a score at 100
# with the ellipsis marker, hiding every digit after the first. The digits
# family is sized for a body of text, not for the column beside a board; this
# cut is drawn for that column instead. Three pixels of ink per digit plus the
# 1px gap is a 4px advance, so the five figures a uint16 score can reach fit
# in 19px and nothing is ever cut off.
#
# The ten digits and nothing else. A string with punctuation in it is not a
# score, and leaving out ':' and '.' also keeps this cut out of the engine's
# auto_font search for clock and temperature strings (see can_stand_in in
# core/src/render.c): a 7px face fills a narrow box better than any cut of the
# digits family, so it would win those boxes and restyle them with a face the
# layout never named.
#
# Every stroke is one pixel, so the cut declares @smooth no: a fitted scale
# stays on whole multiples rather than anti-aliasing a 1px stem into grey.

@name     micro7
@role     digits
@height   7
@baseline 7
@gap      1
@family   micro
@smooth   no

# --- 48 0 ---
48
  |###|
  |#.#|
  |#.#|
  |#.#|
  |#.#|
  |#.#|
  |###|

